<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Redis on cesun.life</title><link>https://example.org/tags/redis/</link><description>Recent content in Redis on cesun.life</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Thu, 03 Sep 2026 15:56:43 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://example.org/tags/redis/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Redis Caching and Coordination Patterns</title><link>https://example.org/redis-overview/</link><pubDate>Thu, 03 Sep 2026 15:56:43 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/redis-overview/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Redis is an in-memory data-structure server that can accelerate reads and
coordinate bounded operations. A production design must define the source of
truth, consistency window, capacity policy, failure behavior, and ownership of
every key. Persistence and replication improve recovery options; they do not
give a cache the transactional invariants of its source database.&lt;/p&gt;
