 &lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;The application reads Redis, loads a miss from the database, stores the result
with a TTL, and returns it. A source write must invalidate or refresh affected
keys. Because database commit and cache mutation are not one atomic operation,
readers can observe stale data between those events. Define the acceptable
window and use a transactional outbox or change-data stream when cache
invalidation must survive process failure.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Redis Data Types and Commands</title><link>https://example.org/redis-commands/</link><pubDate>Tue, 02 Jan 2024 17:43:06 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/redis-commands/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Redis associates each key with one data type. A command issued against a key of
the wrong type fails, so key naming and value ownership should make the data
model explicit.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Redis executes each command atomically on the server thread. Single commands
such as &lt;code&gt;INCR&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;HSET&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;SADD&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;ZADD&lt;/code&gt; are therefore safe for one-key
state changes, but multi-step read-modify-write sequences need &lt;code&gt;MULTI&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;EXEC&lt;/code&gt;,
&lt;code&gt;WATCH&lt;/code&gt;, or Lua if they must behave as one unit.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>